For trees, “due to the cycle-free structure, the edge weight is always equal to the distance of its two endpoints. Therefore, the two equations
\begin{equation}\label{or}
   \frac{d}{dt}\omega_e(t)=-\kappa_e(t)\omega_e(t).
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{or2}
\frac{d}{dt}\omega_e(t)=-\kappa_e(t)d_e(t),
\end{equation}
are identical on trees, and can be transformed into a linear system
\begin{equation}\label{fm1}
  \frac{d}{dt}\omega(t,e)=-\left(\frac{m_2(e)}{m_1(u)}+\frac{m_2(e)}{m_1(v)}\right)\omega(t,e)+\sum_{e_u\sim u,e_u\neq e}\frac{m_2(e_u)}{m_1(u)}\omega(t,e_u)+\sum_{e_v\sim v,e_v\neq e}\frac{m_2(e_v)}{m_1(v)}\omega(t,e_v).  
\end{equation}
when using the weighted Lin-Lu-Yau curvature $\kappa_{\omega}$.”

For the modified (no edge deletion) version, “the existence and uniqueness of the solution can also be established. The proof follows a largely similar approach to the theorem above.”

